//? Title: Recurrent Cells: Gates, Gradients Through Time and Parallel Scans
//? Subtitle: The vanilla RNN, LSTM and GRU checked step by step; how the gradient through time decays and which part of it the gates control; the minimal cells minGRU and minLSTM and the IndRNN, whose gradient is an exact product of scalars; a parallel scan for diagonal recurrences; a latch task and sequential digits, using datamunge's nn::rnn_step, nn::lstm_step, nn::gru_step, nn::linear_scan, nn::mingru, nn::minlstm and nn::indrnn_step
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A recurrent cell maps an input and the previous hidden state to the next state, and a sequence is processed by repeating it. This report checks one step of six cells against their equations, to \(2\times10^{-16}\), and counts their parameters. It then measures how the gradient of the final state with respect to the initial one depends on the sequence length.
For the classical cells the gradient is a product of full Jacobians. The gate bias controls only one path through it, and for the LSTM that path underestimates the gradient by orders of magnitude. For the new minimal cells minGRU and minLSTM, and for the IndRNN, the gradient is exactly a product of gate values, to \(2.4\times10^{-15}\). Because their gates ignore the state, minGRU and minLSTM can be evaluated for all time steps at once with a parallel scan, which agrees with the step-by-step loop to \(4\times10^{-15}\).
The price is expressiveness. On a latch task, where the first of 40 noisy inputs decides the label, every single-layer minimal cell stays at chance while the plain RNN, the LSTM and the GRU with retaining gate biases learn it. Two stacked minGRU layers start to learn it too. On digits read one row at a time, the classical cells reach 91–94% test accuracy, level with the 92% of an MLP that sees the whole image. The best minimal model, two minLSTM layers, reaches 88%.
)

\toc

> The problem

Many inputs are sequences: text, audio, sensor readings, the pixels of an image read in order. A recurrent network keeps a hidden state \(h_t\) and updates it with each input, \(h_t=F(x_t,h_{t-1})\), using the same parameters at every step. Training it by backpropagation through time multiplies one Jacobian \(\partial h_t/\partial h_{t-1}\) per step. Over long sequences that product tends to vanish or explode, so information from early inputs becomes hard to learn from (Hochreiter, 1991; Bengio, Simard and Frasconi, 1994).
The LSTM (Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997; Gers, Schmidhuber and Cummins, 2000) and the GRU (Cho et al., 2014) address this with multiplicative gates that let the state pass through a step almost unchanged. Their gates depend on the previous state, though, so a sequence must be processed one step at a time.
More recent cells drop that dependence to gain parallelism over time. In minGRU and minLSTM (Feng et al., 2024) the gates depend on the input alone. In the IndRNN (Li et al., 2018) each unit sees only its own past.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/recurrent_cells.cpp`. That example checked one step of the RNN, LSTM and GRU against a hand computation and counted their parameters. It measured the gradient with respect to the initial state for sequence lengths up to 80 and several gate biases, and trained the three cells on a latch task. All of that is kept.
Added are the minimal cells, the IndRNN and a parallel scan for diagonal linear recurrences, in `datamunge/nn/recurrent_variants.hpp`. The report also adds closed-form predictions for the gradients, an explanation of why the minimal cells fail the latch task, and a dataset.

> The method

>> The classical cells

The vanilla (Elman) RNN is \(h_t=\tanh(x_tW_x+h_{t-1}W_h+b)\). The LSTM keeps a second state, the cell \(c_t\), and four gates computed from \(x_t\) and \(h_{t-1}\): input \(i_t\), forget \(f_t\), candidate \(g_t\) and output \(o_t\), with

$$
c_t=f_t\odot c_{t-1}+i_t\odot g_t,\qquad h_t=o_t\odot\tanh(c_t),
$$
\alttext(c t equals f t times c t minus 1 plus i t times g t, and h t equals o t times tanh of c t, all elementwise.)

where \(i,f,o\) are sigmoids and \(g\) is a tanh. The GRU has an update gate \(z_t\) and a reset gate \(r_t\), both sigmoids of \(x_tW_x+h_{t-1}W_h+b\), and

$$
n_t=\tanh\big(x_tW_{xn}+(r_t\odot h_{t-1})W_{hn}+b_n\big),\qquad h_t=(1-z_t)\odot n_t+z_t\odot h_{t-1}.
$$
\alttext(n t equals tanh of x t W x n plus r t times h t minus 1, times W h n, plus b n; and h t equals 1 minus z t times n t plus z t times h t minus 1.)

With \(u\) units and \(d\) inputs, the RNN has \(du+u^2+u\) parameters, the GRU three times and the LSTM four times as many. The LSTM's forget bias \(b_f\) and the GRU's update bias \(b_z\) set how much of the state each step keeps at initialisation: about \(\sigma(b)\), which is 0.73 for \(b=1\), 0.95 for \(b=3\) and 0.993 for \(b=5\).

>> The gradient through time

For a loss \(L\) of the final state, \(\partial L/\partial h_0=(\partial L/\partial h_T)\,J_T\cdots J_1\) with \(J_t=\partial h_t/\partial h_{t-1}\). For the RNN, \(J_t=\mathrm{diag}(1-h_t^2)\,W_h^\top\). The library initialises \(W_h\) uniformly on \(\pm\sqrt{3/u}\), so its entries have variance \(1/u\) and \(\mathbb E\lVert vW_h^\top\rVert^2=\lVert v\rVert^2\) for a fixed \(v\).
A mean-field estimate treats the coordinates as exchangeable: each step multiplies the gradient norm by about

$$
\rho_t=\sqrt{\tfrac1u\textstyle\sum_j(1-h_{t,j}^2)^2}\;\lVert W_h\rVert_F/\sqrt u ,
$$
\alttext(rho t equals the square root of the mean over units of 1 minus h t j squared, squared, times the Frobenius norm of W h divided by the square root of u.)

which is below 1 whenever the tanh is not in its linear range, so the gradient vanishes geometrically.

For the gated cells the Jacobian is a sum of paths. In the LSTM, \(\partial c_t/\partial c_{t-1}=\mathrm{diag}(f_t)\) plus terms that go through \(h_{t-1}=o_{t-1}\odot\tanh(c_{t-1})\) into all four gates. In the GRU, \(\partial h_t/\partial h_{t-1}=\mathrm{diag}(z_t)\) plus terms through \(n_t\), \(z_t\) and \(r_t\). Keeping only the diagonal "direct path" gives the estimate \(\partial L/\partial h_0\approx r\odot\prod_td_t\), with \(r=\partial L/\partial h_T\) and \(d_t=f_t\) or \(z_t\). This is the constant error carousel of Hochreiter and Schmidhuber: with the gate near 1, the direct path neither vanishes nor explodes. It is an approximation, and the measurements below show how far it goes.

>> Minimal cells and the IndRNN

minGRU computes its gate and candidate from the input alone:

$$
z_t=\sigma(x_tW_z+b_z),\qquad \tilde h_t=x_tW_h+b_h,\qquad h_t=(1-z_t)\odot h_{t-1}+z_t\odot\tilde h_t .
$$
\alttext(z t equals sigma of x t W z plus b z; h tilde t equals x t W h plus b h; h t equals 1 minus z t times h t minus 1 plus z t times h tilde t.)

minLSTM uses a forget gate \(f_t\) and an input gate \(i_t\), both sigmoids of the input, normalised so that \(f'_t+i'_t=1\): \(h_t=f'_t\odot h_{t-1}+i'_t\odot\tilde h_t\) with \(f'=f/(f+i)\) and \(i'=i/(f+i)\). In minGRU the bias \(b_z\) controls retention through \(1-\sigma(b_z)\), so a negative bias retains: \(b_z=-3\) keeps 0.95 per step. They have \(2(du+u)\) and \(3(du+u)\) parameters.
Both cells are linear in the state, and \(\partial h_t/\partial h_{t-1}\) is the diagonal matrix of the retention gate. The gradient is therefore exactly

$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial h_0}=r\odot\prod_{t=1}^T(1-z_t)\quad\text{(minGRU)},\qquad r\odot\prod_{t=1}^Tf'_t\quad\text{(minLSTM)},
$$
\alttext(dL by dh 0 equals r times the product over t of 1 minus z t for minGRU, and r times the product of f prime t for minLSTM.)

with no other paths. The IndRNN keeps a nonlinearity but makes the recurrent weight a vector, \(h_t=\phi(x_tW+u\odot h_{t-1}+b)\). Its Jacobian is \(\mathrm{diag}(u\odot\phi'(a_t))\), so with ReLU the gradient is \(r\odot\prod_tu\odot\mathbb 1[a_t>0]\). The library draws \(u\) uniformly on \([0,1]\).

>> The parallel scan

A minimal cell's state obeys \(h_t=a_t\odot h_{t-1}+b_t\), with \(a_t\) and \(b_t\) known for all \(t\) before any state is computed. Each step is the affine map \(h\mapsto a\,h+b\), and composing two of them gives another, \((a,b)\circ(a',b')=(a\,a',\ a\,b'+b)\). Composition is associative, so all prefixes can be formed by a scan (Blelloch, 1990).
`nn::linear_scan` uses the Hillis–Steele form. In round \(k\), every position combines its partial result with the one \(2^{k-1}\) steps earlier. After \(\lceil\log_2T\rceil\) rounds every position holds \(h_t\), and the initial state is folded in as \(b_0\leftarrow a_0h_0+b_0\). Each round is a few elementwise operations on \([B,T,H]\) tensors, so the scan has depth \(O(\log T)\) but does \(O(T\log T)\) work, against \(O(T)\) of both for a loop. It is built from differentiable operations, so gradients come for free.

> Simple examples

>> One step of each cell, and parallel against sequential

The program evaluates one step of each cell with \(d=3\) inputs, \(u=4\) units and a batch of 2, recomputes it from the stored parameters by hand, and counts the parameters. It runs minGRU and minLSTM over 50 steps both with the scan and with the step function in a loop. Finally, it times the forward and backward pass of `nn::linear_scan` against a loop for sequence lengths from 8 to 2048.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/recurrent_scan.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
using Vec = std::vector<double>;

// (A W)[i][j] for row-major A [rows, k] and W [k, cols], restricted to columns [c0, c0 + n).
Vec mm(const Vec& A, const Vec& W, int rows, int k, int cols, int c0, int n) {
  Vec out(rows * n, 0.0);
  for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j)
      for (int l = 0; l < k; ++l) out[i * n + j] += A[i * k + l] * W[l * cols + c0 + j];
  return out;
}
double sig(double v) { return 1 / (1 + std::exp(-v)); }
double max_diff(const Vec& a, const Vec& b) {
  double m = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) m = std::max(m, std::abs(a[i] - b[i]));
  return m;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // One step of each cell with in = 3, units = 4, batch 2, against the equations evaluated by hand.
  const int in = 3, u = 4, B = 2;
  nn::Generator g(1);
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({B, in}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64), h = nn::normal({B, u}, 0.0, 0.5, g, F64), c = nn::normal({B, u}, 0.0, 0.5, g, F64);
  const Vec X = x.to_vector<double>(), H = h.to_vector<double>(), C = c.to_vector<double>();
  std::print("| cell | max abs(library − hand) | parameters | formula |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  auto row = [&](const char* cell, double err, const nn::ParameterStore& p, int expect, const char* formula) {
    std::print("| {} | {:.1e} | {} | {} = {} |\n", cell, err, p.count(), formula, expect);
  };
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const Vec out = nn::rnn_step(p, "c", x, h).to_vector<double>();
    const Vec a = mm(X, p.at("c/Wx").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u), r = mm(H, p.at("c/Wh").to_vector<double>(), B, u, u, 0, u), b = p.at("c/b").to_vector<double>();
    Vec hand(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) hand[i] = std::tanh(a[i] + r[i] + b[i % u]);
    row("RNN", max_diff(out, hand), p, in * u + u * u + u, "in·u + u² + u");
  }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const auto st = nn::lstm_step(p, "c", x, {h, c}, 1.0);
    const Vec Wx = p.at("c/Wx").to_vector<double>(), Wh = p.at("c/Wh").to_vector<double>(), b = p.at("c/b").to_vector<double>();
    auto gate = [&](int k) {
      Vec a = mm(X, Wx, B, in, 4 * u, k * u, u), r = mm(H, Wh, B, u, 4 * u, k * u, u);
      for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) a[i] += r[i] + b[k * u + i % u];
      return a;
    };
    const Vec ig = gate(0), fg = gate(1), gg = gate(2), og = gate(3);
    Vec hh(B * u), cc(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) cc[i] = sig(fg[i]) * C[i] + sig(ig[i]) * std::tanh(gg[i]), hh[i] = sig(og[i]) * std::tanh(cc[i]);
    row("LSTM", std::max(max_diff(st.h.to_vector<double>(), hh), max_diff(st.c.to_vector<double>(), cc)), p, 4 * (in * u + u * u + u), "4(in·u + u² + u)");
  }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const Vec out = nn::gru_step(p, "c", x, h, 0.0).to_vector<double>();
    const Vec Wxzr = p.at("c/Wx_zr").to_vector<double>(), Whzr = p.at("c/Wh_zr").to_vector<double>(), bzr = p.at("c/b_zr").to_vector<double>();
    Vec z = mm(X, Wxzr, B, in, 2 * u, 0, u), r = mm(X, Wxzr, B, in, 2 * u, u, u);
    const Vec zh = mm(H, Whzr, B, u, 2 * u, 0, u), rh = mm(H, Whzr, B, u, 2 * u, u, u);
    Vec rH(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) z[i] = sig(z[i] + zh[i] + bzr[i % u]), r[i] = sig(r[i] + rh[i] + bzr[u + i % u]), rH[i] = r[i] * H[i];
    const Vec nx = mm(X, p.at("c/Wx_n").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u), nh = mm(rH, p.at("c/Wh_n").to_vector<double>(), B, u, u, 0, u), bn = p.at("c/b_n").to_vector<double>();
    Vec hand(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) hand[i] = (1 - z[i]) * std::tanh(nx[i] + nh[i] + bn[i % u]) + z[i] * H[i];
    row("GRU", max_diff(out, hand), p, 3 * (in * u + u * u + u), "3(in·u + u² + u)");
  }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const Vec out = nn::mingru_step(p, "c", x, h, -1.0).to_vector<double>();
    const Vec z = mm(X, p.at("c/z/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u), ht = mm(X, p.at("c/h/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u);
    const Vec bz = p.at("c/z/b").to_vector<double>(), bh = p.at("c/h/b").to_vector<double>();
    Vec hand(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) {
      const double zz = sig(z[i] + bz[i % u]);
      hand[i] = (1 - zz) * H[i] + zz * (ht[i] + bh[i % u]);
    }
    row("minGRU", max_diff(out, hand), p, 2 * (in * u + u), "2(in·u + u)");
  }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const Vec out = nn::minlstm_step(p, "c", x, h, 1.0).to_vector<double>();
    const Vec f = mm(X, p.at("c/f/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u), ii = mm(X, p.at("c/i/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u);
    const Vec ht = mm(X, p.at("c/h/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u);
    const Vec bf = p.at("c/f/b").to_vector<double>(), bi = p.at("c/i/b").to_vector<double>(), bh = p.at("c/h/b").to_vector<double>();
    Vec hand(B * u);
    for (int k = 0; k < B * u; ++k) {
      const double ff = sig(f[k] + bf[k % u]), gi = sig(ii[k] + bi[k % u]);
      hand[k] = ff / (ff + gi) * H[k] + gi / (ff + gi) * (ht[k] + bh[k % u]);
    }
    row("minLSTM", max_diff(out, hand), p, 3 * (in * u + u), "3(in·u + u)");
  }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    const Vec out = nn::indrnn_step(p, "c", x, h).to_vector<double>();
    const Vec a = mm(X, p.at("c/W").to_vector<double>(), B, in, u, 0, u), uu = p.at("c/u").to_vector<double>(), b = p.at("c/b").to_vector<double>();
    Vec hand(B * u);
    for (int i = 0; i < B * u; ++i) hand[i] = std::max(0.0, a[i] + uu[i % u] * H[i] + b[i % u]);
    row("IndRNN", max_diff(out, hand), p, in * u + 2 * u, "in·u + 2u");
  }

  // The minimal cells: a whole sequence in parallel against the step function in a loop.
  {
    const Tensor xs = nn::normal({4, 50, in}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64), h0 = nn::normal({4, u}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
    nn::ParameterStore p(2, F64);
    const Tensor pg = nn::mingru(p, "g", xs, u, h0), pl = nn::minlstm(p, "l", xs, u, h0);
    std::vector<Tensor> sg, sl;
    Tensor a = h0, b = h0;
    for (const Tensor& xt : nn::time_steps(xs)) sg.push_back(a = nn::mingru_step(p, "g", xt, a)), sl.push_back(b = nn::minlstm_step(p, "l", xt, b));
    std::print("\nWhole sequence (T = 50) in parallel against 50 calls of the step function: minGRU {:.1e}, minLSTM {:.1e}.\n",
               nn::max(nn::abs(pg - nn::stack_time(sg))).item(), nn::max(nn::abs(pl - nn::stack_time(sl))).item());
  }

  // Cost of the scan against a loop: forward and backward of sum(h ⊙ w), batch 8, 64 channels, median of 5 runs.
  std::print("\n| T | scan rounds | loop (ms) | scan (ms) | ratio loop/scan | max abs difference |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> Ts, tl, ts;
  for (std::int64_t T : {8, 32, 128, 512, 2048}) {
    Tensor a = nn::uniform({8, T, 64}, 0.5, 1.0, g, F64), b = nn::normal({8, T, 64}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
    const Tensor w = nn::normal({8, T, 64}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
    a.requires_grad_(), b.requires_grad_();
    auto loop = [&] {
      Tensor hh = nn::zeros({8, 64}, F64);
      std::vector<Tensor> out;
      const auto as = nn::time_steps(a), bs = nn::time_steps(b);
      for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < T; ++t) out.push_back(hh = as[t] * hh + bs[t]);
      return nn::stack_time(out);
    };
    auto time = [&](auto&& f) {
      std::vector<double> ms;
      for (int r = 0; r < 5; ++r) {
        const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        (void)nn::grad(nn::sum(f() * w), {a, b});
        ms.push_back(std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count());
      }
      std::ranges::sort(ms);
      return ms[2];
    };
    const double l = time(loop), s = time([&] { return nn::linear_scan(a, b); });
    const double diff = nn::max(nn::abs(loop() - nn::linear_scan(a, b))).item();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1e} |\n", T, static_cast<int>(std::ceil(std::log2(T))), l, s, l / s, diff);
    Ts.push_back(std::log2(T)), tl.push_back(std::log10(l)), ts.push_back(std::log10(s));
  }

  auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  pl.lines(Ts, tl, "loop over time", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).points(Ts, tl, "", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6)
      .lines(Ts, ts, "parallel scan", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2).points(Ts, ts, "", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6)
      .x_limits(2.5, 11.5).y_limits(-1.5, 3.5).x_ticks({3, 5, 7, 9, 11}, {"8", "32", "128", "512", "2048"})
      .title("Forward + backward time, batch 8, 64 channels").x_label("sequence length T (log scale)").y_label("log10 time (ms)");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 1);
  fig.add(pl);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/recurrent_scan.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| cell | max abs(library − hand) | parameters | formula |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| RNN | 2.2e-16 | 32 | in·u + u² + u = 32 |
| LSTM | 1.1e-16 | 128 | 4(in·u + u² + u) = 128 |
| GRU | 5.6e-17 | 96 | 3(in·u + u² + u) = 96 |
| minGRU | 1.1e-16 | 32 | 2(in·u + u) = 32 |
| minLSTM | 2.8e-17 | 48 | 3(in·u + u) = 48 |
| IndRNN | 0.0e+00 | 20 | in·u + 2u = 20 |

Whole sequence (T = 50) in parallel against 50 calls of the step function: minGRU 8.9e-16, minLSTM 4.4e-16.

| T | scan rounds | loop (ms) | scan (ms) | ratio loop/scan | max abs difference |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 3 | 0.2 | 0.1 | 1.4 | 8.9e-16 |
| 32 | 5 | 0.7 | 1.1 | 0.6 | 2.7e-15 |
| 128 | 7 | 3.6 | 15.1 | 0.2 | 3.6e-15 |
| 512 | 9 | 20.8 | 51.8 | 0.4 | 2.7e-15 |
| 2048 | 11 | 75.8 | 307.9 | 0.2 | 3.6e-15 |
\image(figures/recurrent_scan.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Time for the forward and backward pass of the diagonal recurrence with batch 8 and 64 channels, as a loop over time (red) and as a parallel scan (blue), on a logarithmic scale.)
\alttext(Both lines rise steadily from about 0.1 ms at T = 8. The blue scan line lies above the red loop line everywhere except at T = 8, where they meet, ending near 90 ms against about 34 ms at T = 2048.)

Every cell agrees with its equations to at most \(2.2\times10^{-16}\), and every parameter count matches its formula: 32 for the RNN, 128 for the LSTM, 96 for the GRU, 32 for minGRU, 48 for minLSTM and 20 for the IndRNN. The scan and the loop give the same 50 states to within \(10^{-15}\).
The timing shows the other side of the scan. On a single CPU, depth does not help. Each of the \(\lceil\log_2T\rceil\) rounds touches the whole sequence, so the scan is slower than the loop beyond the shortest length, by a factor of roughly 2 to 3 in this run. The scan pays off only where many elementwise operations run at once, as on a GPU. Its results agree with the loop to \(4\times10^{-15}\) at every length.

> Checking the theory

>> The gradient through time

For lengths \(T\) from 1 to 80, the program draws random inputs (\(d=4\)), a small random initial state and a random \(r\). It computes \(\partial L/\partial h_0\) (or \(\partial L/\partial c_0\)) for \(L=\sum h_T\odot r\) with 32 units, averaging the norm over 8 initialisations. Along the same trajectories it evaluates the closed forms: the mean-field estimate for the RNN, the direct path for the LSTM and GRU, and the exact products for the minimal cells and the IndRNN.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/recurrent_gradients.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// Columns [c0, c0 + n) of a matrix, or entries of a vector.
Tensor cols(const Tensor& W, std::int64_t c0, std::int64_t n) {
  return W.dim() == 1 ? nn::slice(W, {c0}, {n}) : nn::slice(W, {0, c0}, {W.size(0), n});
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::int64_t in = 4, u = 32;
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> lengths{1, 5, 10, 20, 40, 80};
  enum Kind { Rnn, LstmH, LstmC, Gru, MinGru, MinLstm, IndRnn };
  struct Variant { Kind kind; double bias; std::string label; RGB color; };
  const std::vector<Variant> variants{
      {Rnn, 0, "RNN, ∂L/∂h0", {17, 24, 39}},           {LstmH, 1, "LSTM b_f = 1, ∂L/∂h0", {148, 163, 184}},
      {LstmC, 1, "LSTM b_f = 1, ∂L/∂c0", {220, 38, 38}}, {LstmC, 3, "LSTM b_f = 3, ∂L/∂c0", {234, 88, 12}},
      {LstmC, 5, "LSTM b_f = 5, ∂L/∂c0", {202, 138, 4}}, {Gru, 0, "GRU b_z = 0", {37, 99, 235}},
      {Gru, 3, "GRU b_z = 3", {14, 165, 233}},           {MinGru, 0, "minGRU b_z = 0", {37, 99, 235}},
      {MinGru, -3, "minGRU b_z = −3", {14, 165, 233}},   {MinLstm, 0, "minLSTM b_f = 0", {220, 38, 38}},
      {MinLstm, 3, "minLSTM b_f = 3", {234, 88, 12}},    {IndRnn, 0, "IndRNN, ReLU", {22, 163, 74}}};

  // For each run: ||∂L/∂state0|| for L = sum(h_T ⊙ r), and the norm of r ⊙ prod_t d_t, where d_t is the diagonal
  // "direct path" factor of each step (the whole Jacobian for the diagonal cells, one path among many for the others).
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> measured(variants.size()), predicted(variants.size());
  double worst_exact = 0;
  for (std::size_t v = 0; v < variants.size(); ++v) {
    const auto& V = variants[v];
    for (std::int64_t T : lengths) {
      double m = 0, pr = 0;
      for (std::uint64_t seed = 1; seed <= 8; ++seed) {
        nn::Generator g(seed);
        nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F64);
        const Tensor x = nn::normal({1, T, in}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
        Tensor h0 = nn::normal({1, u}, 0.0, 0.1, g, F64), c0 = nn::normal({1, u}, 0.0, 0.1, g, F64);
        const Tensor r = nn::normal({1, u}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
        h0.requires_grad_(), c0.requires_grad_();
        Tensor hT, factor = nn::ones({1, u}, F64);
        std::vector<Tensor> hs{h0};
        const auto xs = nn::time_steps(x);
        if (V.kind == MinGru || V.kind == MinLstm) {
          const Tensor hseq = V.kind == MinGru ? nn::mingru(p, "c", x, u, h0, V.bias) : nn::minlstm(p, "c", x, u, h0, V.bias);
          hT = nn::reshape(nn::slice(hseq, {0, T - 1, 0}, {1, 1, u}), {1, u});
        } else {
          nn::LstmState s{h0, c0};
          for (const Tensor& xt : xs) {
            if (V.kind == Rnn) s.h = nn::rnn_step(p, "c", xt, s.h);
            else if (V.kind == Gru) s.h = nn::gru_step(p, "c", xt, s.h, V.bias);
            else if (V.kind == IndRnn) s.h = nn::indrnn_step(p, "c", xt, s.h);
            else s = nn::lstm_step(p, "c", xt, s, V.bias);
            hs.push_back(s.h);
          }
          hT = s.h;
        }
        const Tensor gnorm = nn::grad(nn::sum(hT * r), {V.kind == LstmC ? c0 : h0})[0];
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < T; ++t) {
          const Tensor& xt = xs[t];
          const Tensor& hp = hs[std::min<std::size_t>(t, hs.size() - 1)];
          if (V.kind == Rnn) {  // mean-field: sqrt(mean_j tanh'(a_j)²) · ||W_h||_F / sqrt(u) in every coordinate
            const Tensor d = 1.0 - nn::square(hs[t + 1]);
            factor = factor * std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(d)).item()) * std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(p.at("c/Wh"))).item() / u);
          } else if (V.kind == LstmC) {
            factor = factor * nn::sigmoid(nn::matmul(xt, cols(p.at("c/Wx"), u, u)) + nn::matmul(hp, cols(p.at("c/Wh"), u, u)) + cols(p.at("c/b"), u, u));
          } else if (V.kind == Gru) {
            factor = factor * nn::sigmoid(nn::matmul(xt, cols(p.at("c/Wx_zr"), 0, u)) + nn::matmul(hp, cols(p.at("c/Wh_zr"), 0, u)) + cols(p.at("c/b_zr"), 0, u));
          } else if (V.kind == MinGru) {
            factor = factor * (1.0 - nn::sigmoid(nn::matmul(xt, p.at("c/z/W")) + p.at("c/z/b")));
          } else if (V.kind == MinLstm) {
            const Tensor f = nn::sigmoid(nn::matmul(xt, p.at("c/f/W")) + p.at("c/f/b")), i = nn::sigmoid(nn::matmul(xt, p.at("c/i/W")) + p.at("c/i/b"));
            factor = factor * f / (f + i);
          } else if (V.kind == IndRnn) {
            factor = factor * p.at("c/u") * nn::cast(nn::greater(hs[t + 1], nn::scalar(0.0)), F64);
          }
        }
        const Tensor pred = r * factor;
        if (V.kind == MinGru || V.kind == MinLstm || V.kind == IndRnn) worst_exact = std::max(worst_exact, nn::max(nn::abs(gnorm - pred)).item() / std::max(1e-300, nn::max(nn::abs(pred)).item()));
        m += std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(gnorm)).item()) / 8;
        pr += std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(pred)).item()) / 8;
      }
      measured[v].push_back(m), predicted[v].push_back(pr);
    }
  }
  std::print("Mean over 8 initialisations of the gradient norm (and of the direct-path prediction, in brackets):\n\n| variant |");
  for (auto T : lengths) std::print(" T = {} |", T);
  std::print("\n| :--- |");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < lengths.size(); ++k) std::print(" ---: |");
  std::print("\n");
  for (std::size_t v = 0; v < variants.size(); ++v) {
    std::print("| {} |", variants[v].label);
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < lengths.size(); ++k)
      variants[v].kind == LstmH ? std::print(" {:.2e} |", measured[v][k]) : std::print(" {:.2e} ({:.2e}) |", measured[v][k], predicted[v][k]);
    std::print("\n");
  }
  std::print("\nminGRU, minLSTM and IndRNN: largest relative difference between the gradient and r ⊙ ∏ d_t over all runs: {:.1e}.\n", worst_exact);
  std::print("Retention at the gate bias alone: σ(1) = {:.3f}, σ(3) = {:.3f}, σ(5) = {:.4f}; σ(3)^80 = {:.2e}, σ(5)^80 = {:.3f}.\n",
             1 / (1 + std::exp(-1.0)), 1 / (1 + std::exp(-3.0)), 1 / (1 + std::exp(-5.0)), std::pow(1 / (1 + std::exp(-3.0)), 80), std::pow(1 / (1 + std::exp(-5.0)), 80));

  auto panel = [&](std::vector<std::size_t> which, const std::string& title) {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> xs;
    for (auto T : lengths) xs.push_back(static_cast<double>(T));
    for (std::size_t v : which) {
      std::vector<double> xm, ym, xp, yp;  // a zero gradient (IndRNN with every unit switched off once) has no logarithm
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < lengths.size(); ++k) {
        if (measured[v][k] > 0) xm.push_back(xs[k]), ym.push_back(std::log10(measured[v][k]));
        if (predicted[v][k] > 0) xp.push_back(xs[k]), yp.push_back(std::log10(predicted[v][k]));
      }
      pl.points(xm, ym, variants[v].label, variants[v].color, 6);
      if (variants[v].kind != LstmH) pl.lines(xp, yp, variants[v].color, 1.5);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, 84).y_limits(-25, 16).title(title).x_label("sequence length T").y_label("log10 gradient norm");
    return pl;
  };
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(panel({0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, "Classical cells (lines: direct path)"));
  fig.add(panel({7, 8, 9, 10, 11}, "Diagonal cells (lines: exact product)"));
  fig.size(1100, 560).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/recurrent_gradients.svg");
}
```
